   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to antimicrobial resistance\nprevention and education",
    "basePrintNo": "S2191",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2191-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §2803-z, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2191\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to antimicrobial\nresistance prevention and education\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill creates an antimicrobial stewardship program in every hospital\nand nursing home, in order to reduce the development and spread of\ninfections that resist treatment with antibiotics or other antimicrobial\nmedicines.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the public health law by adding a new\nsection 2803-z. Subdivision one of the new Section 2308-z requires an\nantimicrobial stewardship program at every general hospital and nursing\nhome in New York State and provides that each program shall be reviewed\nat least annually and shall incorporate an ongoing process to measure\nthe impact of the program.\n\nSubdivision two requires every general hospital and nursing home to\nensure that appropriate personnel receive antimicrobial resistance\ntraining in addition to other existing infection control training.\nSubdivision three authorizes the This section also authorizes the Health\nCommissioner to promulgate any rules necessary to implement the\nprovisions of the new section.\n\nSection 2 of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAntimicrobial-resistant \"superbugs\" are bacteria and other microbes that\nhave become immune to antibiotics and other antimicrobial medicines, and\nas a result, cause infections that are difficult to cure, and sometimes\ndeadly. The World Health Organization, the United Nations General Assem-\nbly, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the New York\nState Department of Health, and many other major public health organiza-\ntions have recognized antimicrobial-resistant infections as a public\nhealth crisis. In the U.S., nearly 3 million people are infected and as\nmany as 35,000 die every year from antimicrobial-resistant infections,\naccording to the CDC's 2019 Antibiotic Resistance Threats Report.\n\nThis bill establishes an antimicrobial stewardship program at every\ngeneral hospital and nursing home to ensure that proper protocols are in\nplace to stop the spread of antimicrobial-resistant disease. These\nprograms, which will meet or exceed Medicaid and Medicare conditions of\nparticipation for antimicrobial programs in health care facilities, will\nbe reviewed annually and there will be ongoing processes to improve the\nprograms.\n\nThis legislation will reduce the development and spread of antimicrobial\nresistance and antimicrobial-resistant infections in humans and preserve\nthe effectiveness of medically important antimicrobials for treating\ndisease for future generations.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020:S8752 (Kavanagh) - RULES\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law. Effective immediately, any rules and regulations necessary\nto implement the provisions of this act on its effective date are\nauthorized to be made and completed on or before such date.\n",
